    "searchText": "Advanced Reporting and Performance Term 2 2026 summative assessment \n6 \n \nSECTION 2 (42 marks) \nArie \nYou are the financial accountant of Arie plc (Arie) and have responsibility for the \npreparation of Arie’s consolidated financial statements for the year ended 31 January \n2026. Arie has a number of subsidiaries which it has had for several years. Arie prepares \nits financial statements in accordance with IFRS Accounting Standards. The main \nbusiness activities of the group do not include investing in assets or providing finance. \nArie has acquired shares in Champlain Break Ltd (Champlain) in three stages. You need \nto advise on how each of the three acquisitions would be accounted for in the consolidated \nfinancial statements of Arie using the information in Appendix 1. \nYou then have to prepare consolidation journal entries for Champlain using the information \nin Appendix 2. \nThe draft equity section of Arie’s consolidated statement of financial position as at 31 \nJanuary 2026 is in Appendix 3.  \nYou have also been asked to advise on whether the directors of Arie would be correct to \nclassify a future investment in Planter Brock Ltd (Planter) as a joint venture from 1 October \n2026. Information about the investment in Planter is in Appendix 4. \nQUESTIONS: \nQuestion 6 \nAdvise, using the information in Appendix 1, on how each of the three acquisitions of \nshares in Champlain would be accounted for in the consolidated financial statements of \nArie for the year ended 31 January 2026. \nYou should prepare calculations where necessary but are not required to prepare journal \nentries or to calculate goodwill in your response to this question. \n(9 marks) \nQuestion 7 \nPrepare, using the information in Appendices 1 and 2 and showing all workings, any \nconsolidation journal entries necessary to prepare the consolidated financial statements \nof Arie for the year ended 31 January 2026, including the ‘final step’ consolidation \nadjustment. \n(24 marks) \nQuestion 8 \nConstruct, using the information in Appendix 3 and taking into account your answer to \nQuestion 7, the equity section of the consolidated statement of financial position of Arie \nas at 31 January 2026. \nYou must complete this question using the Excel spreadsheet provided. \n(4 marks) \n \n Advanced Reporting and Performance Term 2 2026 summative assessment \n7 \n \nQuestion 9 \nAdvise whether the directors would be correct to classify the future investment in Planter \nas a joint venture. \n(5 marks) \n(Total: 42 marks) \nNotes: \nAll journal entries should be prepared in £’000. \nIgnore the tax effect of any adjustments. \n \n \n \n Advanced Reporting and Performance Term 2 2026 summative assessment \n8 \n \nAPPENDIX 1 (Section 2) \nDetails concerning the acquisition of shares in Champlain \nChamplain has 4,000,000 £1 ordinary shares in issue. \n• \nOn 1 March 2025 Arie acquired 100,000 of the shares for £300,000.   \n• \nOn 1 April 2025 Arie acquired a further 2,300,000 shares at £4.20 per share, the fair \nvalue of each share at that date. \n• \nOn 1 November 2025 Arie acquired a further 400,000 shares for £1,900,000. \nAll changes in fair value have been correctly recorded in Arie’s individual financial \nstatements. \n  \nAPPENDIX 2 (Section 2) \nInformation concerning Champlain \nAccounting policies \n• \nArie measures non-controlling interests at acquisition at a proportionate share of net \nassets. \n• \nDepreciation and gains and losses on disposal of property, plant and equipment are \ncalculated automatically and are included in general and administrative expenses. \nChamplain \n• \nChamplain only has one reserve, retained earnings. The balance of retained \nearnings at the dates of each share acquisition was as follows: \n       \n \n \n \n \n• \nChamplain made a profit of £720,000 for the year ended 31 January 2026. Profits \narose evenly throughout the year. \n• \nOn 1 December 2025 Champlain sold a machine to Arie for £210,000. The machine \nhad a carrying amount of £160,000 and a remaining useful life of four years at 1 \nDecember 2025. Prior to transfer, the depreciation expense was £40,000 per year. \nThe estimated residual value of the machine is nil, and it is depreciated using the \nstraight-line method.  \n• \nAt 31 January 2026, Arie still owed 30% of the amount paid to acquire the machine.  \n \nRetained \nearnings \n \n£’000 \n1 March 2025 \n8,620 \n1 April 2025 \n8,680 \n1 November 2025 \n9,100 \n \n \n Advanced Reporting and Performance Term 2 2026 summative assessment \n9 \n \n \nAPPENDIX 3 (Section 2) \nArie plc \nConsolidated statement of financial position as at 31 January 2026 (Extract) \n \nDraft \nEquity \n£'000 \nShare capital \n23,000 \nShare premium \n4,000 \nRevaluation surplus \n3,780 \nRetained earnings \n23,670 \n \n54,450 \nNon-controlling interests \n2,100 \nTotal equity \n56,550 \n \nAlso provided as an Excel resource \n \nAPPENDIX 4 (Section 2) \nInformation concerning investment in Planter \n• \nArie has agreed to acquire 20% of the ordinary share capital of Planter on 1 October \n2026. \n• \nThere will be two other investors in Planter and their shareholdings in Planter from 1 \nOctober 2026 will be as follows: \n \nInvestor \n% shareholding \nBitan \n50 \nDelton \n30 \n \n• \nUnder contractual arrangements agreed by all three shareholders, key operating and \nfinancial decisions must be agreed by shareholders who together hold at least 85% \nof the shares in Planter. \n• \nUnder the contractual arrangements each investor has rights to the net assets of \nPlanter based on their shareholding in the company. \n \nEND OF SECTION \n \n \n \n",
