    "searchText": "Advanced Reporting and Performance Term 2 2026 summative assessment \n10 \n \nSECTION 3 (19 marks) \nEco \nEco Textiles Pro plc (Eco) is a UK-based company. The main business activity of Eco is \nthe manufacture of clothing. Eco prepares its sustainability report in accordance with the \nGlobal Reporting Initiative (GRI) Standards and its financial statements in accordance \nwith IFRS Accounting Standards. It has a reporting date of 31 December.  \nThe Eco board approved a new sustainability strategy in December 2025 which sets a \ntarget to reduce total water consumption by 20% by 2030. This will result in changes to \nthe business which include closing the main dyeing unit, upgrading existing factories and \ninvesting in advanced water recycling technologies. The changes together will have a \nmaterial impact on business operations and have not yet been communicated to Eco’s \nemployees. \nThe finance director, who is a CA, has insisted that you include a restructuring provision \nin the 2025 financial statements to cover the expected costs of making the changes. \nShe has made it clear that the provision must be included as a signal to shareholders \nthat Eco is taking sustainability seriously, irrespective of the requirements of IAS 37 \nProvisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets, and has implied that your \nannual performance bonus will be impacted if you don’t comply.  \nShe has also asked you to evaluate whether the water consumption section of the draft \nsustainability report provided in Appendix 1 complies with the requirements of GRI 303-\n5 Water Consumption provided in Appendix 2.  \nQUESTIONS: \nQuestion 10 \nAdvise, with reference to IAS 37: \na) \nwhether the planned changes would be a restructuring; and \nb)  \nwhy a restructuring provision should not be recognised at 31 December 2025. \n(6 marks)\nQuestion 11 \nAdvise, with reference to the ICAS Code of Ethics, on the ethical issues for the finance \ndirector arising from her instruction that you recognise a restructuring provision. You are \nnot required to provide any actions. \n(5 marks)\nQuestion 12 \nEvaluate whether Eco’s disclosures in Appendix 1 comply with the requirements of GRI \n303-5, as outlined in Appendix 2. Recommend improvements that could be made to \nensure Eco’s reporting is compliant and useful to stakeholders. \n(8 marks)\n(Total: 19 marks)\n \n \n Advanced Reporting and Performance Term 2 2026 summative assessment \n11 \n \nAPPENDIX 1 (Section 3) \nDraft Sustainability report – Water consumption \nManagement approach \nEco recognises the importance of using water responsibly within our manufacturing \noperations. Water is a key input in our dyeing process, and we are committed to reducing \nour reliance on water resources through improved efficiency and sustainable sourcing \npractices. \nPerformance in 2025 \nIn the year ended 31 December 2025, Eco consumed approximately 2 million cubic \nmetres of water across all production facilities (2024: 2.2 million cubic metres) reflecting \nongoing efforts to enhance water efficiency. \nOperations in water-stressed regions \nEco operates two facilities in areas classified as water-stressed. The water consumption \nin these facilities during 2025 was 20% of total consumption. Eco continues to assess \nthe risks associated with water availability in these areas. \nWater storage \nEco does not currently have water storage facilities. Eco has assessed the potential \nimpacts and determined that water storage is immaterial to its overall water-related \nenvironmental impact because the facilities rely on continuous supply, and storage \nvolumes would represent less than 0.5% of total water consumption.  \nTargets and strategy \nEco has set a target to reduce total water consumption throughout the supply chain by \n20% by 2030. Our strategy includes working with suppliers to encourage water-efficient \npractices and exploring investment in water recycling technologies in our factories.  \n \nAPPENDIX 2 (Section 3) \nGRI 303-5 Water consumption  \nREQUIREMENTS  \na. \nTotal water consumption from all areas in megaliters.  \nb. \nTotal water consumption from all areas with water stress in megaliters.  \nc. \nChange in water storage in megaliters, if water storage has been identified as \nhaving a significant water-related impact.  \nd. \nAny contextual information necessary to understand how the data have been \ncompiled, such as any standards, methodologies, and assumptions used, including \nwhether the information is calculated, estimated, modelled, or sourced from direct \nmeasurements, and the approach taken for this, such as the use of any sector-\nspecific factors.  \n \nEND OF ASSESSMENT \n \n",
